    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe following Markdown renders strangely and perhaps wrongly in a hover inside Neovim:\n\n```\nYou can use empty values (and even cast multiple times) to change\nwhat is considered an empty value:\n\n    # Using default\n    iex> params = %{title: \"\", topics: []}\n    iex> changeset = cast(%Post{}, params, [:title, :topics])\n    iex> changeset.changes\n    %{topics: []}\n    \n    # Changing default\n    iex> params = %{title: \"\", topics: []}\n    iex> changeset = cast(%Post{}, params, [:title, :topics], empty_values: [[], nil])\n    iex> changeset.changes\n    %{title: \"\"}\n```\n\nNeovim renders the `[:title, :topics]` array as a link:\n\n<img width=\"821\" height=\"266\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/acf7e631-60e1-4186-8bf7-78e406844a8e\" />\n\nBut it should be rendered as its original raw text because it's inside an [indented code block](https://www.markdownlang.com/basic/code.html#markdown-code-blocks) and not a normal paragraph. Here's an example from Zed:\n\n<img width=\"636\" height=\"374\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cb050ced-6671-4273-ae00-ca5e3c0ffa97\" />\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nConcrete example with Expert, an Elixir language server:\n\nEnsure Expert is installed and you're in a minimal Phoenix application:\n\ninit.lua:\n```lua\nvim.pack.add({\n  { src = \"https://github.com/neovim/nvim-lspconfig\" },\n})\n\nvim.lsp.config('expert', {\n  capabilities = capabilities,\n  cmd = { 'expert', '--stdio' },\n  root_markers = { 'mix.exs', '.git' },\n  filetypes = { 'elixir' },\n})\nvim.lsp.enable 'expert'\n```\n\nGenerate the Phoenix app so you get the Ecto stuff that we want docs for:\n```\nmix local.hex\nmix archive.install hex phx_new\nmix phx.new my_app\n```\n\nOpen my_app/lib/my_app.ex and add some code to hover over. Hover over `cast`:\n```\ndefmodule MyApp do\n  @moduledoc \"\"\"\n  MyApp keeps the contexts that define your domain\n  and business logic.\n\n  Contexts are also responsible for managing your data, regardless\n  if it comes from the database, an external API or others.\n  \"\"\"\n    require Ecto.Changeset\n    Ecto.Changeset.cast\nend\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe indented code blocks should be treated like fenced ones, so links should not appear there. `[]` should just be printed verbatim.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno (there is no hover there without external LSP support)\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nArch Linux\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nKonsole\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nArch extra repo",
